# Environment files (contains sensitive API keys)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Firebase configuration variants generated with concrete project values
# The tracked lib/firebase_options.dart reads local env/dart-define values and
# intentionally does not contain committed Firebase project keys.
lib/firebase_options_*.dart

# Firebase native config files (google-services.json, GoogleService-Info.plist)
# are intentionally tracked. They DO each contain an API key (`current_key` /
# `API_KEY`) — an earlier version of this comment said they did not, which is
# false and is the premise that left two secret-scanning alerts open for 10+
# months. The correct reason to track them: a Firebase client key is not a
# secret in Google's model. It is compiled into every shipped APK/IPA and can be
# extracted from any install, so confidentiality was never the control. The two
# controls that are real:
#   1. API key restrictions in the Google Cloud Console (Android: package name +
#      SHA-1; iOS: bundle ID) — this is what actually bounds abuse.
#   2. firebase/firestore.rules + firebase/storage.rules, both auth-required and
#      owner-scoped on every path.
# Rotation is deliberately NOT the remediation (owner decision, 2026-09-10): the
# keys are in git history across five paths, so rotating changes which key is
# live without removing anything from history. See todos/360.
# The iOS plist is bundled into the app via Xcode Resources; removing it would
# break the iOS build. The Android json is kept for consistency (the Gradle
# plugin is disabled, so it is not used at build time, but it is the canonical
# record of the Firebase project IDs).

